WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.326
Modern .

2
00:00:01.326 --> 00:00:13.394
NET applications often receive configuration from containers, orchestrators, cloud platforms, serverless settings, managed services, external configuration stores, and CI/CD systems.

3
00:00:13.394 --> 00:00:20.289
These layers are powerful, but they also expand the places where security-sensitive behavior can change.

4
00:00:20.289 --> 00:00:25.395
Environment variables are useful, but they are not automatically secret-safe.

5
00:00:25.395 --> 00:00:35.407
They may be visible through platform tools, process inspection, logs, crash dumps, deployment history, or support exports depending on the environment.

6
00:00:35.407 --> 00:00:43.032
Use them intentionally and prefer platform secret mechanisms or managed identities when they better match the risk.

7
00:00:43.032 --> 00:00:53.508
Cloud secret stores, managed identities, workload identities, and platform configuration services can reduce direct secret handling when configured carefully.

8
00:00:53.508 --> 00:00:57.022
The goal is not merely to move values somewhere else.

9
00:00:57.022 --> 00:01:03.918
The goal is to control access, audit use, support rotation, and keep runtime identities narrowly scoped.

10
00:01:03.918 --> 00:01:13.598
CI/CD systems carry deployment credentials, service connections, signing material, storage credentials, package tokens, and environment variables.

11
00:01:13.598 --> 00:01:22.682
Those values should be protected with least privilege, approval workflows, branch protections, environment separation, and access review.

12
00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:27.986
A pipeline identity should not have broader rights than the deployment requires.

13
00:01:27.986 --> 00:01:35.412
Configuration review belongs in pull requests, release approvals, infrastructure changes, and incident response.

14
00:01:35.412 --> 00:01:39.523
A code change can be safe but paired with risky configuration.

15
00:01:39.523 --> 00:01:45.291
An infrastructure update can change runtime behavior without touching application code.

16
00:01:45.291 --> 00:01:50.198
Review should include both intended values and where they will be applied.

17
00:01:50.198 --> 00:01:53.712
Detect drift between intended and effective settings.

18
00:01:53.712 --> 00:02:02.464
Backups, rollback plans, rotation history, change history, and monitoring help teams understand how configuration changed over time.

19
00:02:02.464 --> 00:02:07.769
Secure configuration is maintained continuously, not only at initial deployment.
